Say what the matching machinery is doing, on the path the root nub takes

The panel of run 453 showed the device tree being read for the first time --
the root nub had a name and the tree held the UFS node -- and then it showed
the matching starting and stopping, with not one line about candidates: no
"M<n>" line, which probeCandidates prints on entry, and no "nio: C" line,
which it prints for every candidate it instantiates.  Both of those strings
are in the kernel image, so the matcher did not run; it did not run badly, it
did not run.

The reason the panel could not show this is in the shape of the code.  The
probe that used to stand there, "nio: DSM enter", sits in the branch of
startMatching() that runs when the match is synchronous.  The root nub is not
matched synchronously.  It is matched on the configuration thread: the job
that carries the match is queued with _IOServiceJob::startJob() and picked up
by a thread that waits on a semaphore.  Every line that could have shown the
match going wrong was on the other branch, so the asynchronous path was
silent from end to end.

This patch puts the whole path in sight, and it says which of the three
places the match can stop is the one that stopped:

  nio: CFG enq <nub> out=<n>          the job was queued (in pingConfig)
  nio: CFG wait                       the configuration thread is alive
  nio: CFG pick <nub>                 the configuration thread took the job
  nio: DSMi <nub> n=<count>           doServiceMatch() ran, with this many
                                      candidates for this nub
  nio: cand <k> cls=.. nm=.. pc=..    what is in the bucket, name match
                                      included
  nio: DSMx <nub>                     that round of matching ended

The candidate line is the one that settles the personality question.  The
catalogue holds one bucket per provider class, and the bucket a nub is
offered is chosen by walking the nub's metaclass chain.  For the root nub
that chain ends in IOPlatformExpertDevice, which is where the platform
expert's personality and the storage driver's root personality both land, so
the two of them are offered together and the name each one asks for is
printed next to it.  Whether "device-tree" or the root nub's own name is the
right string is answered by the panel instead of by an argument about the
device tree's compatible property.

Nothing here changes what the kernel does.  Every addition is a printf on a
path that already runs, and the prints are the short ones the panel can
hold.

---

